<font size="3"><b>Snow!</b></font><br>
<i>Posted 3/7 by peter</i><p><p>
<p>
We finally had skiable snow at the farm last night.  We went skiiing down The Brook Road this morning early while it was blowin' and snowin'.  At Burned Out Batman we went to look at the brook.  It was really pretty with all the snow on the hemlocks and the brook moving along noisily. We were tired by the time we got home.<br>
<br>
Stove wood is all put up three tiers deep x 7' high in the main wood shed.  All the cut down piles along that median stone wall in the long field are now cut-up, split, and stacked.  Warmth enough for "a hundred winters morning milkings", as Rosemary put it years ago.<br>
<br>
The little, old "treatment room" at the top of the stairs now has book shelves clear the floor and is filled with books.  The rest of the room, however, is open and suitable for meditations.<br>
The computer has moved from the living room up to Simon's old room which is evolving into Dedie's office.<br>
<br>
Carl and Peggy are back in Cambridge after their week vacation in New Orleans and Florida.  Carl suffered from a flu for several days while away but is in fine shape again now.  We are very eager to see our old partners and friends and exchange small talk and large as we have for so many good years.<br>
<br>
Sounds like Steve is doing wonderfully in England.  That museum sounds incredible with its fabulous collections of skeletons and skins from around the globe.  Steve, how are the mechanics of data collection working?  Have you backed up any data yet?  Emailed any to your friends yet?  Birchington, like Hardwick, sounds like a well-kept secret.<br>
<br>
Everyone please stay in touch.<br>
<br>
Pete and Dedie<p>
